[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"1985","stub":{"id":"1985","section":"Hooes Rd. (Route 636) to Lorton P.O. (Route 1)","altname":"","river":"Pohick Creek","difficulty":"IItoIIIplus","states":["USA-VRG"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":{"status":"below-recommended","latestReading":{"value":"32","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:50:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null},"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":null,"id":"112340","location":{"latitude":"38.7569999694824","longitude":"-77.2268981933594"},"name":"Put In","distance":"0","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"takeout","description":null,"id":"117622","location":{"latitude":"38.7005004882812","longitude":"-77.209098815918"},"name":"Take Out","distance":"5.5","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[{"id":"wAvnDUEjYWBDRZUXZcxv6","title":"New  impassable sieve ","post_date":"2021-08-15 18:55:38+00","detail":"A trail footbridge that was installed July 2021 has created and impassable sieve. It is 2 miles downstream of the tradition put in and 75 yards downstream of the alternative put in. See comments for details.","user":{"name":"Ned Howenstine"}}],"photos":[],"detail":{"id":"1985","permitUrl":null,"permitInfo":null,"description":"Pohick Creek is formed near Burke, and flows for 13 miles to Pohick Bay. It is flat and braided for its first 5.5 miles, down to Hooes Road (the traditional put in). Because it is pretty flat for the next 2 miles as well, which usually also contain some fallen trees, paddlers found a downstream put in, amongst the new housing developments. Take Rolling Road to Northampton Road (heading west), and then turn left onto Pleasant Lake Drive. At the bottom of the hill is a sign and paved footpath into the park; about 300 yards carry brings you to the creek.\n\n There are three particularly memorable rapids. Double Z (class III+) comes less than a mile from the put in, after several class II and II+ rock gardens. You have to make a very sharp left, right, left. With about a foot of water, the rapid is actually easier, because the route widens out and you can bounce through holes instead of crashing into rocks. Pohick Falls (class III) comes soon thereafter and is more straightforward, but it is easy to flip in the turbulence at the bottom, where the creek is funneled into a narrow chute. It too is less technical at higher levels. Lorelei Ledge (class II+), almost at the end of the trip, is wide and often scrapey; at higher levels it becomes class III because of the hole that forms at the bottom (except on a more gradual route on the far left).\n\n Strainers can be a problem, but Jeff Davis organizes periodic strainer removals (one was in April 2002), which help immensely. The scenery is generally pleasant, although houses are encroaching. And donÂt miss the graffiti art gallery beneath I-95.\n\n If you donÂt stop to scout or play, this trip takes only about one hour. But if you head down there after work from DC, allow more time than that for the drive on I-95. Below US 1, the creek continues for two miles, at 10 feet/mile, to Pohick Bay, which opens into Gunston Cove and then the Potomac River.\n\nEd Evangelidi testifies:<br>\n\nA classic small creek intermediate run with everything from tight Z-turn rapids in the upper section to river-wide ledges in the lower stretch. Strainers and concrete footpaths thrown in too. Although there isnÂt a trail along the whole way down, the safety factor is good here as there are trails along most of the way to get out in an emergency or scout around an obstruction. If the creek has lots of water, try the harder and more continuous South Run of Pohick.","averageGradient":"33","maxGradient":"110","length":"5.6","correlations":[{"reachID":"1985","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecom